<h2>What a parish register is</h2>
<p>Until 1918, births, marriages and deaths in the Russian Empire were recorded by the church — or the mosque, synagogue or Lutheran parish. Every parish kept a register (метрическая книга): a baptism with the birth date, parents and godparents; a wedding with the age and estate of bride and groom; a burial with the age and cause of death. A second copy went to the diocese, and today it is usually in the state archive of the region.</p>

<h2>How to find the right register</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Find the village and uyezd as they were at the time</strong> — not on today's map. Governorates, uyezds and volosts changed; villages were renamed.</li>
  <li><strong>Find the parish</strong> — which church the village belonged to. The parish map, old lists of settlements and the archive's inventories help.</li>
  <li><strong>Find the archive</strong> — usually the state archive of the region the uyezd lay in. In its guide, look for the fond of the diocesan consistory or of the church itself.</li>
  <li><strong>Look for scans online.</strong> Many archives publish their registers, and FamilySearch has filmed a huge share — search its catalog by place name. If there are no scans, write to the archive.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How to read one</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Old Style dates.</strong> Dates are Julian: add 12 days in the 19th century and 13 in the 20th for the modern date. Keep the date as written in the tree and note the calendar.</li>
  <li><strong>Two dates.</strong> A baptism record usually gives the day of birth and the day of baptism — don't mix them up.</li>
  <li><strong>Church names.</strong> Иоанн is Ivan, Феодор is Fyodor. Peasants' surnames were often not written at all — just the name and the father's name.</li>
  <li><strong>Confession lists and revision lists</strong> list whole households — who lived together and roughly how old they were.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to keep in the tree</h2>
<p>Archive, fond, opis, delo, list (for example "GATO, f. 160, op. 1, d. 2345, l. 12 ob."), the entry number and its text as written. Then any relative can open the same page, and in ten years you will still know where the date came from.</p>
